History: why a released APK used to be GPL-3.0
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Kept for anyone building from, or auditing, a commit before the Gemini Nano
migration -- not a statement about the current build.

The app's native library (`libdigitalpet-native.so`) used to link espeak-ng,
licensed GPL-3.0-or-later, and the app bundled espeak-ng's phoneme data under
`android/app/src/main/assets/espeak-ng-data/`. espeak-ng was used by Piper
for text-to-phoneme conversion; the pet could not speak without it.

Apache-2.0 is one-way compatible with the GPL: Apache-2.0 code may be
combined into a GPL-3.0 work, and the result is GPL-3.0. Consequently any
binary that linked espeak-ng -- including a released APK built from one of
those commits -- had to be distributed under GPL-3.0, with the corresponding
source and licence text made available to recipients.

The obligation attached to linking, not to calling. Piper did provide a
non-espeak phonemizer (`PhonemeType::TextPhonemes`), but choosing it changed
nothing while espeak-ng remained in the link, and nearly every published
Piper voice was an espeak-phoneme model.

The ESP32-S3 firmware was never affected -- it never linked espeak-ng, and
the audio it plays arrives over BLE already synthesised, then and now.

